# Context

## Background
After an upgrade, each agent session that was open keeps its ohya mcp
process on the old build. Only ohya doctor reported that. The owner
ran install.sh, saw "memory: ready", and had no sign that two sessions
still ran the deleted binary (2026-11-25). docs/installer-plan.md
asks setup to report external running clients that need a new session.

## Why This Way
- The result is a warning. Report.Complete() is false only for failed,
  unsupported, and blocked, so a warning keeps exit code 0 and the
  upgrade ends well. Setup must not stop a coding session: the owner
  decides when a session reconnects.
- setup calls the mcp_build check of doctor. One rule for "another
  build" serves both commands.
- setup reads the processes before setup.Run. The verification step of
  setup starts an ohya mcp process of the setup binary. With a
  committed build that process counts as "this build". With an
  uncommitted build the check cannot tell it from another build, so it
  would give a false warning when the process is still alive. Before
  the run, that cannot happen.
- setup reads <state>/observe, or <state>/join/observe for a remote
  connection, the directory that ohya mcp publishes to in each mode.
- The result prints after the run. When setup.Run returns an error,
  printSetupReport prints the failure and no result, as for the PATH
  conflict. Doctor then reports the processes.

## Gotchas
- runSetupCommandWith takes the flags without the command name.
- A remote setup without --dry-run pairs with a hub, so a remote test
  uses --dry-run.
- The text test runs the real path through a shell wrapper around the
  test binary ("child-run"), the pattern of TestRemoteSetupPairsAndResumes.
- 9 mutation checks failed a test, and a tenth (a missing result) was
  repeated in a form that compiles. The placement before setup.Run is
  a design decision without a test: the verification process is reaped
  before the run ends in both placements.

